        <div id="formats" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Standalone, Rack Attachment or Bench Attachment</h2>
<p>
            The first decision is the format, and it is mostly decided by what you already own.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Standalone machines</strong> are the most comfortable and the quickest to use:
            you sit down, set the roller and go. They also take the most floor — the RitFit is about
            51 by 44 inches, the Titan 42 by 36 — and you need somewhere to put it. Choose one if leg
            isolation is a regular part of your training and you have the space.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Rack attachments</strong> hang inside a power rack and borrow its frame. They
            add almost nothing to your footprint, which is their whole appeal, and they are usually
            cheaper than a full machine. The trade-offs are compatibility — they fit only certain
            uprights — a standing rather than seated or lying curl, and a lower capacity: the Titan
            Series attachment is rated to 275 lb of plates and a 265 lb user. If you are choosing a
            rack, our guide to <a href="/best-squat-racks-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">squat racks for a home gym</a> explains upright sizes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Bench attachments</strong>, often called leg developers, bolt or pin onto the end
            of a weight bench. Simple versions are cheap and fiddly; better ones, like REP's, have a
            cam, a proper roller system and their own wheels. They suit a gym built around a good
            bench, but you have to swap the attachment on and off, and they only fit specific benches.
            Our guide to the <a href="/best-weight-benches/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best weight benches</a> covers the benches themselves.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="plate-vs-selectorized" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Plate-Loaded vs Selectorized</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Plate-loaded machines</strong> use the Olympic plates you already own, cost far
            less, and weigh a fraction as much — the Titan standalone is 108 lb and the RitFit 109 lb.
            The trade-off is time and fiddliness: changing load means sliding plates on and off a
            short post, which is slow for drop sets or for two people of different strengths sharing
            a machine. Plates on short posts can also shift or rattle during reps; Garage Gym Reviews
            noticed this on one machine and suggested a collar.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Selectorized machines</strong> carry their own weight stack: you move a pin and
            the load changes in seconds. They feel smooth and consistent, suit shared home gyms and
            fast supersets, and are what most commercial gyms use. They also cost several times as
            much and weigh a great deal: Body-Solid's Pro Select weighs 412 lb assembled with its
            standard 210 lb stack, and its weight stack is fixed, so a very strong lifter can run out
            of plates on the stack. Plan delivery and placement carefully; a selectorized machine is
            not something you move around a garage.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you already own a full set of plates and want one machine, plate-loaded is the
            sensible default. If several people share the gym, you train with fast supersets, or you
            simply hate loading plates, a selectorized machine may be worth the price. Our <a href="/best-weight-plates/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight plates guide</a> covers what to buy if you are starting from nothing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cams-and-rollers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Cams, Cables and Rollers: How the Resistance Is Delivered</h2>
<p>
            Two machines can carry the same plates and feel completely different, because the
            mechanism between the plates and your shins changes the resistance through the rep.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A simple lever</strong> with a plate post on the end, as on most leg developers
            and the Titan standalone, follows an arc: the effort changes as the lever angle changes,
            often feeling easiest at one end of the movement and hardest in the middle. BarBend
            describes the resistance on leg developers as moving in an arc that starts easier, gets
            harder to about halfway and eases off at the top. It works, and it is simple and durable.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A cam</strong> — an off-centre pulley — changes the effective lever arm through
            the movement, so the resistance can be shaped to match how strong the quads are at each
            knee angle. Body-Solid's Pro Select uses a 7-gauge steel cam; REP describes the cam on
            its bench attachment as keeping tension consistent from start to finish.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A cable and pulley</strong>, as on the RitFit PLC01, gives more even tension
            through the rep than a plain lever. Garage Gym Reviews preferred it to a hinge-type machine
            for that reason. Note the ratio: RitFit's is 3:2, so the plates you load feel lighter
            than their face value. As arithmetic from that ratio, 75 lb of plates feels like about 50
            lb, which also means the 375 lb plate capacity is roughly 250 lb of effective resistance.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The roller pads</strong> matter more than they look. Large, firm rollers spread
            the load over your shins; thin ones dig in at heavy loads. Garage Gym Reviews found the
            pads on the RitFit thin and expected them to compress over time. If extensions hurt your
            shins, BarBend's tip of wearing high-top shoes and setting the roller over the tongue is
            worth trying before blaming the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="knee-alignment" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Knee Alignment and Fit</h2>
<p>
            The single most important fit check on any leg extension is whether the machine's pivot
            point lines up with your knee. The machine's arm rotates around its pivot; your lower leg
            rotates around your knee joint. When the two axes line up, the roller stays in the same
            place on your shin through the rep and the movement feels smooth. When they do not, the
            roller slides up and down your shin and the movement can grind or pull.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Set the seat depth first.</strong> Sit all the way back, with the back pad against your lower back. The pivot of the machine's arm should sit level with the side of your knee. On machines with a sliding back pad or seat — the Titan has seven seat depth positions — move it until it does. Long and short thighs need very different settings.</li>
            <li><strong>Set the roller.</strong> The extension roller should rest on the front of your lower shin, just above the ankle, not on your foot. Body-Solid quotes a 13 to 17 inch range from seat to roller on the GLCE365, which is a quick way to check fit if you know your own lower-leg length.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the range of motion.</strong> Machines with a range-of-motion adjustment, such as the Body-Solid Pro Select's rotary start position, let you begin the rep with less knee bend, which some people find more comfortable.</li>
            <li><strong>Mind the height range.</strong> RitFit says the PLC01 suits users from 5 feet 2 inches to 6 feet 2 inches. Lifters outside a maker's stated range should ask before buying.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Bench attachments and rack attachments are the formats most likely to fit poorly,
            because the seat position is set by the bench or the rack, not by the machine. BarBend
            notes that buyers with particularly short or long thighs may struggle on a leg developer
            whose seat cannot move. If you can, sit on a machine of the same design before buying.
          </p>
          <p>
            The leg extension's reputation as bad for the knees mostly comes from heavy, jerky reps
            and poor set-up. Controlled reps with a well-aligned machine are a mainstay of knee
            rehabilitation. If you have an existing knee problem, follow your clinician's advice on
            load and range; our article on <a href="/are-treadmills-bad-for-your-knees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether treadmills are bad for your knees</a> covers how load and knees interact more generally.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compatibility" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Bench and Rack Compatibility</h2>
<p>
            Attachments save space and money, but only if they fit what you own. The makers are
            unusually specific about this, and it pays to believe them.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>REP's bench attachment</strong> fits the REP BlackWing, Nighthawk and AB-5000 ZeroGap adjustable benches, with standard or wide pads. REP says it does not fit the AB-5200 or the original 5200, and it cannot guarantee non-REP benches.</li>
            <li><strong>Titan's TITAN Series rack attachment</strong> fits TITAN Series racks and metric 3 by 3 inch uprights — 2.95 inches square with 26 mm holes on 50 mm spacing. Titan states plainly that it will not fit imperial 3 by 3 uprights. Titan sells a separate version for its T-3 and X-3 racks, the one carded here because it is the version sold on Amazon.</li>
            <li><strong>Body-Solid's GLDA3 leg developer</strong>, according to Body-Solid and its Amazon listing, fits only Body-Solid's GFID71 and GFID100 benches.</li>
            <li><strong>Marcy's leg developer</strong> fits its MWB-5511 and MWB-7096 benches.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Independent descriptions do not always match. BarBend describes the Titan Series rack
            attachment as fitting any rack with 3 by 3 inch uprights and one-inch holes, which is
            broader than Titan's own wording, so go by the maker. Measure your uprights and hole
            diameter with calipers if you are unsure, rather than trusting the nominal size.
          </p>
          <p>
            REP's attachment, which is sold direct, also takes an optional cable kit that links it to a cable machine, so
            you can use a weight stack you already own. Northern Fitness lists the minimum distance from
            a cable trolley as about 43 inches. If you are weighing a cable machine purchase, our guide to <a href="/best-cable-machines-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cable machines for a home gym</a> covers the options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="seated-or-lying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Seated or Lying Leg Curl?</h2>
<p>
            Most combination machines pair the extension with a hamstring curl, and they do it in
            one of two ways. The Body-Solid Pro Select offers a seated curl from the same seat, as
            Titan's machine (sold direct) does, and the Body-Solid GLCE365 a reclined one; the RitFit uses a lying, prone curl, face down
            on the pad; and the Titan rack attachment uses a standing curl.
          </p>
          <p>
            The difference is more than comfort. In a seated curl your hips are flexed, which puts
            the hamstrings, which cross both the hip and the knee, on more stretch. A 2021 study that
            compared the two, cited by Garage Gym Reviews in its RitFit review, found greater
            hamstring growth with seated than lying curls over the training period, though both
            worked. If hamstring development matters to you, a machine with a seated curl has an
            edge; if you mainly want quads with some hamstring work, either is fine.
          </p>
          <p>
            Lying curls have their own advantages: they are easier to set up on a bench attachment,
            and some lifters find them more comfortable for the lower back. Whichever you choose,
            train the hamstrings as seriously as the quads; an extension-only routine builds an
            imbalance around the knee.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="checklist" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Before You Buy a Leg Extension Machine</h2>
<ol>
            <li><strong>Choose the format from what you own.</strong> A compatible rack or bench makes an attachment the obvious choice; otherwise budget for a standalone machine.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the plate hole size.</strong> The Titan and RitFit take 2-inch Olympic plates; RitFit says the PLC01 also works with 1-inch plates. Body-Solid's GLCE365 has a 1-inch post with an optional Olympic adapter sleeve.</li>
            <li><strong>Read the capacity label.</strong> User weight, plate capacity and combined capacity are different numbers. A 265 lb user limit, as on the Titan rack attachment, excludes some heavier lifters.</li>
            <li><strong>Measure with room to get in and out.</strong> Allow space beside the machine to load plates, and in front for the curl position.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the warranty.</strong> Body-Solid's in-home lifetime warranty is the longest here; Titan lists one year on both its products.</li>
            <li><strong>Protect the floor.</strong> A machine and its plates concentrate weight on small feet. Our guide to <a href="/best-gym-flooring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym flooring</a> covers what to put under it.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            A leg extension is an accessory, not the core of leg training. Pair it with a compound
            movement: our guides to <a href="/best-leg-press-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">leg press machines</a> and <a href="/best-hip-thrust-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">hip thrust machines</a> cover the next pieces, and our <a href="/home-workout-equipment-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home workout equipment overview</a> puts them in order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Leg Extension Machine</h2>
          <p>Most leg extension regrets come down to fit, compatibility or misreading a number.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying an attachment that does not fit</h3>
              <p>Rack and bench attachments are made for specific uprights or benches, and nominal sizes can mislead: Titan's TITAN Series attachment fits metric 3x3 uprights but not imperial 3x3. Check the maker's compatibility list against your exact rack or bench model, and measure hole size and spacing if in doubt.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ignoring the pulley ratio</h3>
              <p>On a cable machine with a 3:2 ratio, the plates you load feel lighter than their face value, so you need more plates than you expect and the headline capacity overstates the effective resistance. Count your plates before ordering, or budget for more.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Skipping the knee alignment set-up</h3>
              <p>Jumping on and lifting without adjusting the seat depth and roller is the quickest way to make a good machine feel bad. Line the pivot up with the side of your knee and set the roller just above the ankle before the first working set, and recheck if someone else uses it.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Comparing capacities that measure different things</h3>
              <p>One maker quotes user weight, another plate capacity, another the two combined, and independent sources sometimes print a different figure again. Read the label on every number, and if a figure matters to you, ask the maker rather than relying on a review table.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">For most home gyms, a cable machine such as the RitFit PLC01 from RitFit's store, or
            LIONSCOOL's on Amazon, is the best-value leg extension machine</strong>, and XMark's rotary leg extension and curl machine is the one to choose for more
            adjustment. If you already own a Titan T-3 or X-3 rack or a compatible Body-Solid bench, an
            attachment saves floor space; if several people share the gym, Body-Solid's selectorized Pro
            Select is the convenient, expensive option.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whichever you buy, check compatibility against your exact rack or bench, read what each capacity
            figure measures, and line the pivot up with your knee before you load it. Then pair it with a
            compound leg movement — <a href="/best-squat-racks-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">our squat rack guide</a> is the place to start.
          </p>
        </div>

